or is even slightly better (noise level). The worst case for
meta-only is D4 SDNET ($-4.0$ pp vs blend), where the L1 structural
invariants about concrete cracks carry specific content the
Controller cannot reconstruct from routing rules.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Limitations}
\label{ssec:ctrl_limits}

Each AD-mode test item pays an extra VLM call (Controller), which
adds approximately $35$--$50\%$ wall-time over v10 depending on
domain. The learning procedure is semi-supervised: the $40$-item dev
label budget per domain is the cost of rule construction (Stages B--C);
on top of that the reused domain-rule pilot consumes a further
$K{=}10$ balanced FN/FP labels per domain for its L2 corrective rules
(Appendix~\ref{app:rulebook_pilot}). Only the reference-only L1
invariants are genuinely label-free. The rulebook is fixed at training
time; online rule evolution from test feedback is future work. The
v11 floor guarantee depends on the Controller's JSON being
parseable; when it is not we fall back to the v10 blend rather than
either branch alone (observed parse-failure rate on this run: $0\%$).
Finally, the D9 regression flags a genuine limitation of
disagreement-based learning: when both branches are predominantly
wrong together, the Controller has no routing signal to exploit.

